With ``--vectorize-linear`` (or ``re2c:vectorize:linear`` configuration) |re2c|
looks for *linear chains* in the DFA: sequences of states where every state has
exactly one single-character transition that continues the chain. Such chains
appear when the input is dispatched on long fixed strings, for example HTTP
header names, tag or attribute names, or file signatures. At the first state of a
chain the generated lexer reads the next 2, 4 or 8 code units at once and
compares them with the characters on the chain. If they are equal, it skips them
and jumps to the last state of the chain; otherwise it continues with the
ordinary one-character dispatch. The fast path is taken only when the ordinary
dispatch would follow the same transitions, so the matched rule, the match
length and the cursor are the same as without the option.

The option helps when the lexer spends its time on long literals that are
often matched to the end. It does not help keyword recognition in a lexer that
also has an identifier rule: every prefix of a keyword is then an accepting
state, and the chain stops there, so the generated code is usually the same as
without the option. On short tokens that rarely match a literal, the extra
comparison may cost more than it saves.

The multi-character primitives are defined by the following code templates in
the syntax file (see the section about syntax files):

``code:yypeekn``
    An expression that yields the value of the ``n`` code units at the cursor,
    where the code unit at offset ``k`` occupies bits ``8*k`` to ``8*k+7``. The
    literal that the value is compared with uses the same layout, so the value
    does not depend on the byte order of the host. Supported variables: ``n``,
    ``YYINPUT``, ``YYCURSOR``, ``yyrecord``, and the list variable ``offset``
    that enumerates code unit offsets from ``0`` to ``n-1``.

``code:yypeekn_guard``
    A condition that must hold before ``code:yypeekn`` is evaluated, or an empty
    string if no check is needed. Supported variables: ``n``, ``YYINPUT``,
    ``YYCURSOR``, ``YYLIMIT``, ``yyrecord``.

``code:yyskipn``
    A statement that advances the cursor by ``n`` code units. Supported
    variables: ``n``, ``YYINPUT``, ``YYCURSOR``, ``yyrecord``.

The C and Go syntax files define them for all APIs. With the simple and record
APIs the value is assembled from single bytes of the input, which compilers
turn into one load. With YYFILL the reads stay within the input reserved by
the preceding ``YYFILL`` check (the usual ``YYMAXFILL`` padding contract). Without
YYFILL the reads are guarded by a bounds check: in C it uses ``YYLIMIT`` (which
must then be defined and point after the last readable code unit), in Go it
uses the length of ``YYINPUT``. With the generic API the user defines
``YYPEEKN(cursor, n)`` and ``YYSKIPN(cursor, n)``; ``YYPEEKN`` must not read
past the end of input and must return zero if fewer than ``n`` code units are
available (chains made only of zero code units are never coalesced). The syntax
files of the other languages call a user-defined ``YYPEEKN(cursor, n)`` with
every API, under the same contract, and leave ``code:yypeekn_guard`` undefined.

The optimization applies only to the ASCII encoding, where a code unit is one
byte (``YYCTYPE`` must be one byte wide). It is disabled with the end-of-input
rule ``$``, because then ``YYFILL`` does not reserve input in advance. Chains do
not pass through accepting states or states with tag operations, ``YYFILL``
checks or backtracking save points, but they may end in such a state, for
example in the accepting state of a literal.
